      "view": {
        "mode": "single",
        "stem": "A circle has area $S=\\pi r^2$ and positive radius $r$. Use $\\pi\\approx3.14$ for areas $S_1\\approx12.5\\,\\mathrm{cm}^2$ and $S_2\\approx8.24\\,\\mathrm{cm}^2$. Which complete result gives $r$ in terms of $S$, both table-derived radii in order, and the radius multipliers when the area is multiplied by $4$ and by $\\frac1{25}$, respectively?\n\nFor these items, use the following transcribed Table IV cells from Bradis's Four-figure Mathematical Tables (2010). Decimal commas are written as decimal points. A row N below 10 uses main-column digit j for argument N+j/100 and correction-column digit k for the extra argument k/1000. A row N at least 10 uses j/10 and k/100 instead. Add 0.001 times the correction-cell integer to the main root entry. A dash means no correction is needed. Normalize any out-of-range positive argument as a*10^(2m), with 1 <= a < 100, and scale the table root by 10^m.\n\n| Row N | Main column j | Main root entry | Correction column k | Correction integer |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 2.6 | 2 | 1.619 | 4 | 1 |\n| 3.9 | 8 | 1.995 | 1 | 0 |\n\nFor this item, round S/3.14 to four significant figures before addressing the table. Apply the captured correction and report the returned positive radius to three decimal places in cm. This input-rounding policy is a condition of this item.",
        "figure_ids": [],
